window.docContents[179].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials":{"contents":"A generalized exponential φ is specified by a recurrence and an initial\nvalue, together with strict monotonicity and continuous differentiability on\n[0,1]; its inverse ψ is called a generalized logarithm.  The first formulas\nstate these defining relations and the simplest identity choice.  Later\nformulas continue that choice across the negative and positive real axes using\nthe principal real logarithm and iterated exponentials or logarithms.\n\n\n\nThe reusable interfaces package the recurrence, normalizations, continuation\nbranches, strict monotonicity, and continuous differentiability without making\neach displayed formula assume its own conclusion.\n\n\n\n\n\n\\varphi(x+1)=\\exp(\\varphi(x)),\\qquad -1<x<\\infty.The generalized exponential satisfies this recurrence for every real x in\nthe interval (-1,∞).\n\nThe phrase “inverse generalized logarithm” is represented by a paired\ninterface: its leftInverse and rightInverse fields assert the two\ncomposition identities explicitly.\n\n\n\nThe generalized exponential and logarithm are strictly increasing on the\nclosed unit interval, and each is continuously differentiable there (indeed,\nthe interface records global ContDiff regularity).\n\n\n\n\\varphi(0)=0.The normalization fixes the generalized exponential at the origin.\n\n\\psi(e^x)=1+\\psi(x),\\qquad -\\infty<x<\\infty.The inverse generalized logarithm obeys the corresponding unit-shift relation\nfor every real x.\n\n\\psi(0)=0.The generalized logarithm has the matching origin normalization.\n\n\\varphi(x)=\\psi(x)=x,\\qquad 0\\le x\\le1.The simplest admissible choice takes both functions to be the identity on the\nunit interval.  The Lean form permits the two functions to be named while\nmaking both identity equalities explicit.\n\n\\varphi(x)=\\ln(x+1),\\qquad -1<x<0.On the interval (-1,0), the simplest generalized exponential continues as\nthe principal real logarithm of x+1.\n\n\\varphi(x)=\\underbrace{\\exp\\cdots\\exp}_{\\lfloor x\\rfloor\\ \\mathrm{times}}\n(x-\\lfloor x\\rfloor),\\qquad x>1.For x>1, let ⌊x⌋ be the natural-number floor.  Apply the ordinary\nexponential exactly that many times to the fractional part x−⌊x⌋; the\nresult is the generalized exponential.\n\n\\psi(x)=e^x-1,\\qquad -\\infty<x<0.The inverse continuation on the negative half-line is the ordinary\nexponential shifted down by one.\n\n\\psi(x)=\\ell+\\underbrace{\\ln\\cdots\\ln}_{\\ell\\ \\mathrm{times}}x,qquad x>1.For x>1, the generalized logarithm is a positive integer ℓ plus ℓ\nsuccessive principal logarithms.  The existential Lean statement records that\npositive integer rather than hiding it in an opaque choice.\n\n0\\le\\underbrace{\\ln\\cdots\\ln}_{\\ell\\ \\mathrm{times}}x<1.The positive integer ℓ is selected so that the ℓ-fold logarithm lands in\nthe half-open unit interval.  Every intermediate input to those principal\nlogarithms must remain positive; this domain guard prevents the totalized\nvalue of Real.log 0 from creating a second, spurious count.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.12 Generalized Logarithms and Exponentials","header":"§4.12 Generalized Logarithms and Exponentials","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials--___4___12-Generalized-Logarithms-and-Exponentials"}});